        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_Springs%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The chapter emerged in the late 1930s when the Navajo Nation formally established the Rock Springs Chapter under the Eastern Navajo Agency.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rock_springs_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Rock Springs lies within the historic 'Checkerboard Area,' a patchwork of land ownership created by the 1887 Dawes Act.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rock_springs_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early growth in Rock Springs was driven by nearby coal mines, a railroad for coal transport, and a natural gas pipeline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rock_springs_new_mexico",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Water from the area flows through Burned Death Wash, which feeds into the Little Colorado River watershed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_Springs%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Rock Springs Chapter covers the communities of Sagebrush, China Springs, and Rock Springs itself, spanning over 41,000 acres.",
